# tend root for run `{{run_id}}` — preset `{{preset}}`

Generated by `cf plan-run`. Do not hand-edit: regenerate instead, so `run.yaml` keeps
recording the prompt shas that were actually used.

| | |
|---|---|
| entrypoint | `{{entrypoint}}` |
| gate (out of tree) | `{{gate_dir}}/verify_findings.py --gate-config {{gate_dir}}/gate.json` |
| changed-files check | `{{gate_dir}}/check_changed_files.py --base {{merge_target_branch}}` |
| bridge | `{{bridge}}` |
| model | `{{model}}` |
| source access | `{{book_access}}` |
| sources | `{{sources_inline}}` |
| tasks | {{task_count}} |
| concurrency | {{max_workers}} workers / {{max_reviewers}} reviewers |

`prompts/` holds the rendered worker/reviewer prompts; `bin/` holds the bridge scripts
(`scripts/run/` in the skill); `.tend/` holds the `tend-agent` fallback configuration, used only
when the bridge is `tend-agent`.

The gate takes the worker's task id from `$TEND_TASK_ID` and refuses a contribution that rewrites
another task's findings file. Whether tend exports that variable into a validation command's
environment is **unverified**: read the gate's `changed files:` line on the first task of the smoke
test below — `ownership checked against task <id>` means it does, and `ownership: single-stem rule`
means it does not, and only the weaker rule (one task's pair per contribution) is running.

Start with a **1/1 smoke test** and watch one task through worker → gate → reviewer → merge
before raising the limits:

```bash
uv run tend-control limits --root {{tend_root}} --workers 1 --reviewers 1
uv run tend run --root {{tend_root}} --log-level INFO
# ... one task all the way through ...
uv run tend-control limits --root {{tend_root}} --workers {{max_workers}} --reviewers {{max_reviewers}}
```

Set a budget ceiling before raising concurrency.
